  "technical_issues": [
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "The reported 600 km, 97.79° near-circular orbit is broadly plausible for a sun-synchronous orbit, but a specific local solar time of the ascending/descending node is not given. Therefore the assertion that the descending track is constantly sunlit and the claimed illumination conditions are not established."
    },
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "RAAN is not determined by the launch facility's location. The statement that the initial RAAN indicates the launch facility is incorrect; injection geometry, launch epoch, and the selected orbital plane determine the initial RAAN."
    },
    {
      "where": "Launch",
      "issue": "The report selects Vega E while acknowledging it had not been built. This is not a demonstrated available launch option; rideshare eligibility, deployment orbit compatibility, schedule, and capability are not substantiated."
    },
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "The report gives no sensor specification, ground sample distance, spectral capability, swath, or underwater-performance analysis to show that a satellite at 600 km can provide useful fluid-lensing reef imagery or the stated monitoring products."
    },
    {
      "where": "Ground Control & Communications",
      "issue": "The access table contains a duplicated access entry and reports a maximum contact of about 12.8 minutes, not an average of almost ten minutes. It does not establish data volume or link-budget adequacy."
    },
    {
      "where": "Ground Control & Communications",
      "issue": "A near-polar orbit does not by itself ensure long contact for the vast majority of passes; contact depends on station latitude, elevation mask, orbit geometry, and ground-station antenna constraints."
    },
    {
      "where": "Mission Duration & EOL",
      "issue": "The report describes an EOL deorbit manoeuvre but also states the satellite has no thrust capability. A drag sail is a passive disposal device, not an orbital manoeuvre; no deployment design or decay-time estimate is provided."
    },
    {
      "where": "Mission Duration & EOL",
      "issue": "A five-year useful lifespan is asserted without supporting spacecraft reliability, power, or consumables analysis. The text also suggests solar panels may lengthen life without explaining the limiting-life mechanism."
    },
    {
      "where": "Bibliography",
      "issue": "Reference [6] is Wikipedia, which the assignment explicitly disallows as a proper source. Citations numbered [49]–[74] in the launch tables do not have corresponding bibliography entries."
    }
  ],
  "overall": "A coherent Australian reef-monitoring concept is presented, with useful STK material and coverage of most required mission sections. The strongest limitations are unverified payload feasibility, flawed orbital and EOL reasoning, speculative launch selection, and incomplete citation and figure/table presentation."
